0 ~Batima block 2 x 2 - hollow studs, conical sockets, cast stone
0 Name: batima-2x2.dat
0 Author: spex project (geometry read off photographs of the real object)
0 !LDRAW_ORG Unofficial_Part
0 !LICENSE Licensed under CC BY 4.0 : see CAreadme.txt
0 // MODELLED FROM PHOTOGRAPHS OF REAL BATIMA BLOCKS, not from a patent
0 // drawing and not from a modern brick. Stefan photographed a set with its
0 // albums: "BATIMA - Le Jeu de Construction ideal - ALBUM des Numeros
0 // 1-2-3-4-5-6-7 (le numero 6 = 750 blocs; le numero 7 = 900 blocs) -
0 // Brevete", with Dutch text on the same sheets, so Belgian and French/Dutch
0 // - consistent with Le Batima Soc. and FR 588985, granted 1924.
0 //
0 // THE DATE 1924 IS THE PATENT'S, NOT THE OBJECT'S, and the sources do not
0 // agree on the object: architoys.net says 1900-10, a PBA Galleries lot says
0 // c. 1905, MoDIP's image says c. 1930, and the Belgian MOT catalogues the
0 // albums as 1900-1949. The film should therefore label the number as a
0 // patent and not as a birthday.
0 //
0 // AND THERE IS A SECOND, DIFFERENT BATIMA. Focus on Belgium describes
0 // Batima building bricks introduced in 1954 by Nazaire Beeusaert of Deinze,
0 // made of WOOD, with studs on top and TUBES underneath - thirty years
0 // later, another material, and the very mechanism this act says arrives in
0 // 1958. Same name, different toy. Recorded here because a caption is
0 // exactly where that confusion would land.
0 //
0 // WHAT THE PHOTOGRAPHS SHOW, and why the previous placeholder was wrong in
0 // every particular:
0 //
0 //   THE STUDS ARE HOLLOW. Every stud is a raised RING with a bore straight
0 //   down its middle. A LEGO stud is a solid cylinder. This is the single
0 //   most visible difference and it is on every block in every photograph.
0 //
0 //   THE UNDERSIDE IS A FIELD OF CONICAL SOCKETS, one per stud position -
0 //   countersunk cups, each with a small hole at the bottom. There is no
0 //   tube, no wall clutch, and no hollow shell: the coupling is a cone
0 //   entering a cone. It is a different mechanism from 1940 and 1949, not a
0 //   worse version of the same one.
0 //
0 //   THE MATERIAL IS DISPUTED AND THIS FILE DOES NOT SETTLE IT. Cream and
0 //   terracotta, speckled and weathered, and in the photographs it reads as
0 //   porous cast stone. But the Museum of Design in Plastics' own image and
0 //   a PBA Galleries lot both call Batima "the earliest known plastic
0 //   construction toy", compression moulded from CASEIN FORMALDEHYDE -
0 //   Galalith. architoys.net says "composition, wood, card"; one dealer
0 //   says "terre cuite". Four sources, three materials. Whatever else, the
0 //   colour is what it is: `batima.ldr` binds 503 Very_Light_Grey, dE 10.1
0 //   from the photographed cream; 422 Sienna_Brown (dE 7.7) is the
0 //   terracotta if the shot ever wants it.
0 //
0 // PROPORTIONS ARE MEASURED OFF THE PHOTOGRAPHS AS RATIOS OF THE STUD
0 // PITCH, which is the one number a caliper still has to supply. The
0 // pitch is kept at LDraw's 20 LDU so this block stands beside the other two
0 // at a comparable size, which is what A3-S02 and A3-S03 compare. The height
0 // is 20 LDU rather than a brick's 24, because the real block reads flatter
0 // than a LEGO brick against its own width. Four numbers would replace every
0 // estimate here and none of them needs more than a caliper: stud pitch,
0 // block height, stud outer diameter, bore diameter.
0 //
0 // NO `BFC CERTIFY`: the body quads are hand-wound and an unverifiable
0 // certification is worse than none.
0 //
0 // LDraw primitives used under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 licence.
0 // LDraw is a trademark of the LDraw Organization.

0 // ---- body: a plain block, 40 x 40 LDU footprint, 14 LDU tall.
0 //      LDraw +Y down, so the top face is y = 0 and the bottom is y = 14.
4 16 -20 0 -20 20 0 -20 20 0 20 -20 0 20
4 16 -20 14 -20 20 14 -20 20 14 20 -20 14 20
4 16 -20 14 -20 20 14 -20 20 0 -20 -20 0 -20
4 16 20 14 20 -20 14 20 -20 0 20 20 0 20
4 16 20 14 -20 20 14 20 20 0 20 20 0 -20
4 16 -20 14 20 -20 14 -20 -20 0 -20 -20 0 20

0 // ---- four hollow studs, outer radius 6, bore radius 2, 4 LDU tall.
0 //      MEASURED as ratios of the stud pitch off the photographs:
0 //      stud 0.60, bore 0.19. At the 20 LDU pitch this file keeps, that is
0 //      12 and 3.8 - rounded to 12 and 4 so the top annulus is expressible
0 //      as ring1 + ring2 at scale 2, spanning exactly 2 to 6.
1 16 -10 0 -10 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 batima-stud.dat
1 16 10 0 -10 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 batima-stud.dat
1 16 -10 0 10 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 batima-stud.dat
1 16 10 0 10 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 batima-stud.dat

0 // ---- four sockets in the underside, aligned with the studs above.
0 //      Mouth 0.65 of the pitch = 13 LDU, rounded to 12 for the same reason.
1 16 -10 14 -10 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 batima-socket.dat
1 16 10 14 -10 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 batima-socket.dat
1 16 -10 14 10 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 batima-socket.dat
1 16 10 14 10 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 batima-socket.dat
